      <memberdef kind="function" id="_marmot_monomial_basis_functions_8h_1af9d593a9f0e207edd06fcbad8c93d3ba" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="yes" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>void</type>
        <definition>void Marmot::Math::computeMonomialBasis</definition>
        <argsstring>(int order, const Eigen::VectorXd &amp;x, Eigen::VectorXd &amp;res)</argsstring>
        <name>computeMonomialBasis</name>
        <qualifiedname>Marmot::Math::computeMonomialBasis</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>int</type>
          <declname>order</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>const Eigen::VectorXd &amp;</type>
          <declname>x</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>Eigen::VectorXd &amp;</type>
          <declname>res</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Evaluate the complete monomial basis <formula id="95">$ \boldsymbol{H}(\boldsymbol{x}) $</formula> of order <formula id="77">$ n $</formula>. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>The entries are all monomials <formula id="96">$ x_1^{\alpha_1} \cdots x_d^{\alpha_d} $</formula> with <formula id="97">$ \alpha_1 + \dots + \alpha_d \le n $</formula>, ordered with the exponent of the last coordinate varying slowest and that of the first coordinate fastest. For example, in 2D with <formula id="98">$ n = 2 $</formula>: <formula id="99">$ \boldsymbol{H} = [1,\ x_1,\ x_1^2,\ x_2,\ x_1 x_2,\ x_2^2]^T $</formula>; in 3D with <formula id="100">$ n = 1 $</formula>: <formula id="101">$ \boldsymbol{H} = [1,\ x_1,\ x_2,\ x_3]^T $</formula>. The first entry is always <formula id="102">$ 1 $</formula>.</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">order</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Completeness order <formula id="77">$ n $</formula>. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">x</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Coordinates <formula id="5">$ \boldsymbol{x} $</formula> (the dimension <formula id="4">$ d $</formula> is taken from its size). </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="out">res</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Basis vector; must already have the size computeSizeOfMonomialBasisVector( order, d ). </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
